RSV Infection / Bronchiolitis
Respiratory System
Infant coryza, Expiratory wheezing, Chest retractions, Poor feeding

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-042
Reason for Visit: Rapid deterioration of breathing in an infant with a viral cold, characterized by wheezing, chest sucking in, and refusal to feed, requiring urgent pediatric respiratory assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Infant patient and both parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a 6-month-old infant exhibiting increased work of breathing, intercostal retractions, and drastically reduced wet nappies. Urgent assessment was required to evaluate for impending respiratory exhaustion and severe dehydration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "He has had a runny nose for days, but tonight his breathing is fast and noisy, his ribs are sucking in, and he won't take his bottle at all."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms began 4 days ago with clear rhinorrhea and coryza, initially suspected to be a mild upper respiratory tract infection. Over the past 24 hours, the parents noted a low-grade fever and progressive tachypnea with a prolonged expiratory phase. The infant has developed audible expiratory wheezing. Crucially, there has been poor feeding and outright refusal of the bottle for the last 8 hours due to the severe respiratory distress. The parents report visible intercostal and subcostal retractions.
Functional Status: Lethargic infant, unable to maintain normal feeding routine.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety in first-time parents; significant risk of rapid dehydration.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 mmHg | HR: 155 bpm | Temp: 37.8 C | RR: 55 breaths/min | SpO2: 93% on room air | Wt: 7.5 Kg
General: Infant appears fatigued, pale, and irritable. Obvious increased work of breathing.
Physical Exam: Respiratory examination reveals marked tachypnea (RR 55). Moderate intercostal and subcostal retractions are visible, along with mild nasal flaring. Auscultation demonstrates widespread expiratory wheezing and diffuse fine crackles bilaterally. Cardiovascular exam reveals tachycardia. Assessment of hydration shows slightly sunken fontanelle and dry mucous membranes, confirming the reported poor feeding.
Environmental Observations: Humidifier running in the room; parents holding the infant upright.
Medication Reconciliation: Infant paracetamol administered 4 hours ago.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: RSV Infection / Bronchiolitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Age of the patient (infants rarely have classic asthma), presence of coryza/fever, and diffuse fine crackles which indicate bronchiolar involvement rather than pure bronchospasm.

Bacterial Pneumonia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of high spiking fever, toxic appearance, or focal unilateral consolidation signs.

Croup - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of the classic barking seal-like cough or inspiratory stridor; the signs here are strictly lower respiratory (wheeze/crackles).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The infant presents with the classic clinical progression of RSV bronchiolitis: an initial upper respiratory prodrome evolving into lower respiratory tract inflammation characterized by wheezing, crackles, and significant tachypnea. The presence of intercostal retractions and nasal flaring indicates a high work of breathing. The most critical factor is the refusal to feed, which, combined with the tachypnea, has led to early clinical dehydration. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would pose an unacceptable risk of respiratory exhaustion, apnea, and severe dehydration requiring intensive care admission. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly required to physically assess the work of breathing, evaluate hydration status, and establish a safe feeding and monitoring plan.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered saline nasal drops and nasal suctioning to clear upper airway resistance. Advised frequent, small volume feeds. Oxygen saturations are borderline (93%) but acceptable for home management provided feeding improves.
Positive Behavioral Change: Administer 10mL of formula or breastmilk using an oral syringe every 15 minutes instead of offering a full bottle; providing these tiny, frequent amounts physically prevents the baby's stomach from becoming too full to breathe deeply, directly guaranteeing they stay hydrated without causing respiratory exhaustion.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Bulb syringe for nasal suctioning.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review tomorrow morning to reassess work of breathing and weight/hydration status; low threshold for pediatric emergency admission.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate Emergency Department presentation (call 000) if the infant has rapid breathing with pauses longer than 15 seconds (apnea), if the lips or tongue turn blue, if the baby becomes floppy and difficult to wake, or if there are no wet nappies for 12 hours.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Bronchiolitis is a viral illness; antibiotics and asthma inhalers are generally ineffective and not recommended.
Clinical Course: The breathing difficulty typically peaks around day 3 to 5 of the illness before slowly improving over 2 to 3 weeks.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call for an ambulance immediately if your baby's skin sucks in deeply under their ribs with every breath, or if they start grunting loudly every time they breathe out.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The virus has caused the tiny breathing tubes deep in the lungs to swell and fill with mucus; we cannot cure the virus, so our entire focus must be on keeping the nose clear and gently pushing fluids into the baby to prevent dehydration.
Actionable Steps: Use the saline drops and gently suction the nose exactly 5 minutes before every feed; babies can only breathe through their noses while drinking, so clearing this physical blockage directly allows them to swallow without suffocating.

